seg-3f27662a983e = <b>Read or write files.</b> Content is inline: inputs are text in the request, capped at { "4" } MiB, and a scenario field that names a file is refused. Only the <c1/> tools touch a folder, and only the one named by <c2/>, with every path confined to it.

# text
# c1: kshana osnma verify <file> --public-key ... --json
# c2: osnma_verify
seg-3efe81d26cad = <b>Verify OSNMA.</b> There is no MCP tool: the input is a long raw receiver stream (I/NAV pages or a UBX capture), which suits the CLI and Python, and a tool taking one would add a large-input surface for no benefit. Send the user to <c1/> (or Python <c2/>). Whatever its output says, carry the advisory verbatim: authentication status only, not a navigation integrity service, not type-approved navigation equipment, and no anti-spoofing performance claim; a result is meaningful only with a Merkle root or public key the user obtained by another route.

